# GnuTLS -- the TLS stack, and the last package of the crypto wave.
#
# Packaged for 802.1X: enterprise Wi-Fi with client certificates, which is what
# NetworkManager's Network panel needs to connect to a corporate network.
# NetworkManager selects a crypto backend at build time and is built
# -Dcrypto=gnutls.
#
# NOT FOR wpa_supplicant, AND THE DISTINCTION IS WORTH WRITING DOWN because the
# two sit next to each other in the Wi-Fi story and an earlier draft of this
# file got it backwards. wpa_supplicant's CONFIG_TLS defaults to openssl and
# pkgs/wpa_supplicant deliberately leaves it there -- EAP-TLS, PKCS#12 and the
# SAE group arithmetic all go through openssl, against a version bounded below
# 4. Nothing in that package links gnutls. If this recipe is ever reconsidered,
# the consumer to check is NetworkManager, not the supplicant beneath it.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
# BLFS 12.4 pins 3.8.10.
SRC_URL=https://www.gnupg.org/ftp/gcrypt/gnutls/v3.8/gnutls-3.8.10.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=db7fab7cce791e7727ebbef2334301c821d79a550ec55c9ef096b610b03eb6b7
SRC_DIR=gnutls-3.8.10

# ==============================================================================
# THE TWO BUNDLED COPIES, AND WHY THE DEFAULTS ARE ALREADY RIGHT
# ==============================================================================
# gnutls ships lib/minitasn1 and lib/unistring -- its own copies of libtasn1 and
# libunistring -- behind --with-included-libtasn1 and --with-included-unistring.
# Both default to "no", and BOTH FAIL LOUDLY rather than silently vendoring:
#
#   libtasn1     pkg-config check; on failure sets included_libtasn1=yes and
#                then nine lines later converts that into as_fn_error,
#                "Libtasn1 was not found. To use the included one, use
#                --with-included-libtasn1"  (configure:13804)
#   libunistring LINK-PROBES u8_normalize in -lunistring and, on failure,
#                as_fn_error "Libunistring was not found..."  (configure:21868)
#
# So neither switch is passed: the default already refuses to vendor, and
# stating it would only restate a default that is not at risk. What IS worth
# recording is that an earlier reading of this file assumed the opposite --
# that a missing libtasn1 would quietly fall back to minitasn1 -- which is the
# usual shape and is not what this package does. install.sh asserts the outcome
# in the built library anyway, since "no bundled copy" is a claim about the
# artefact rather than about the flags.
#
# THIS IS ALSO WHY libunistring IS PACKAGED AT ALL. BLFS calls it "recommended";
# with the book's own configure line it is a hard requirement.
#
# ==============================================================================
# --with-default-trust-store-pkcs11="pkcs11:"
# ==============================================================================
# Where GnuTLS gets its CA anchors: from p11-kit's trust module, over PKCS#11,
# rather than from a file path compiled in here. That keeps ONE trust store for
# the system -- p11-kit already carries the compiled-in path to
# ca-certificates' bundle, and this makes GnuTLS read the same one.
#
# The alternatives are --with-default-trust-store-file and
# --with-default-trust-store-dir. Either would work and both would be a SECOND
# source of truth about which CAs are trusted, disagreeing with p11-kit the
# first time one is updated and not the other.
#
# The failure mode if this is wrong is the one this whole wave is written
# against: a GnuTLS that builds, links, negotiates TLS and trusts nothing --
# or worse, silently falls back to an empty store and reports every certificate
# as untrusted. install.sh asserts the URI is compiled into the library.
#
# ==============================================================================
# COMPRESSION: link, NOT THE dlopen DEFAULT
# ==============================================================================
# --with-zlib, --with-brotli and --with-zstd each take yes/link/dlopen/no, and
# the default is `yes` which configure then REWRITES TO dlopen when dlopen is
# available (configure:92057). zlib, brotli and zstd are all packaged here, so
# the default would work -- by dlopening them at runtime by soname.
#
# `link` is named instead so the dependency is VISIBLE. A dlopen dependency
# carries no DT_NEEDED, so nothing in the artefact records it: no linkage check
# sees it, the package would install on a system without brotli, and RFC 8879
# certificate compression would simply not be available with no error anywhere.
# Linking makes the three libraries show up where install.sh and any future
# audit can see them, and makes a missing one a build failure rather than a
# runtime absence.
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE REST
# ==============================================================================
# --without-idn. try_libidn2 defaults to YES (configure:89870), so this is an
# auto that would switch itself on the day libidn2 is packaged for some other
# reason. libidn2 is not packaged today, so the default resolves off by
# accident; naming it makes IDNA a decision. THE COST IS REAL AND WORTH
# STATING: without it, internationalised domain names are not converted to
# A-labels before certificate name checking, so a certificate for an IDN host
# will not match. Nothing in the current desktop needs that; if it ever does,
# the fix is to package libidn2 and flip this, not to discover it in the field.
#
# --without-tpm and --with-tpm2=no. trousers and tpm2-tss are not packaged.
# Both would otherwise be probed for.
#
# --disable-cxx, AND THE BUILD IS WHAT FOUND THIS. gnutls builds libgnutlsxx,
# a C++ binding, by default. The first build here produced:
#
#     WARNING: undeclared dependency on gcc (linked: libgcc_s.so.1,
#     libstdc++.so.6); add it to [dependencies] or the package will install
#     and not run
#
# The two ways out are to declare gcc as a runtime dependency, as libsass, llvm
# and cmake legitimately do, or to stop building the thing that needs it.
# Nothing in this distribution consumes the C++ binding: NetworkManager builds
# -Dcrypto=gnutls against the C API, and so does glib-networking. Shipping it
# would add a gcc dependency to every consumer of gnutls in exchange for an ABI
# nothing calls.

#
# --disable-tests. The test suite is compiled by default and is large; nothing
# runs it here, because _scripts/build-meson.sh's autotools sibling never
# invokes make check. Build time only -- nothing is installed either way.
#
# NOT PASSED: --disable-static. Unlike libtasn1 and nettle in this same wave,
# gnutls reports "--enable-static[=PKGS] build static libraries [default=no]",
# so static is already off and the flag would restate a libtool default rather
# than pin anything. install.sh asserts no .a ships regardless, which is the
# check that actually matters.
#
# NOT PASSED: --enable-openssl-compatibility. It installs a libgnutls-openssl
# shim for software written against OpenSSL's API. Nothing here uses it, and it
# is a second ABI to keep working.
#
# NOT PASSED: --enable-ssl3-support, --enable-sha1-support. Both re-enable
# things removed for good reason; SHA-1 as an acceptable certificate digest is
# not something to switch on to make an old certificate work.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--with-default-trust-store-pkcs11=pkcs11: \
--with-zlib=link --with-brotli=link --with-zstd=link \
--without-idn --without-tpm --with-tpm2=no --disable-cxx \
--disable-tests --docdir=/usr/share/doc/gnutls-3.8.10"
